# Expectation record for 05_pointers/009_null_semantics.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 009_null_semantics
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Null pointer constant forms, comparison, truth value and conversion, using 0 and (void *)0 only
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
No null pointer is dereferenced anywhere in this program. Every dereference is guarded,
either by a preceding null comparison, by the guard inside takes_pointer, or by the pointer
having just been assigned the address of a live object. The two null pointer constant
spellings used are the integer constant 0 and the cast form (void *)0, which are exactly
the forms the standard defines as null pointer constants. The stddef.h null-pointer macro
is deliberately NOT used because it comes from a header and no header may be included; the
literal token does not appear anywhere in the source. The null function pointer is compared
and tested for truth but NEVER called. The partly initialized static table has its
unmentioned members null by static initialization, which is guaranteed, not assumed.
No signed integer overflow, no shift out of range, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. The only external symbol is printf; no header is included,
no file, socket, environment variable, clock or random source is touched, and every input
is a literal in this source file.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Nothing about the null pointer's representation is printed or relied upon: only equality
results, truth values and conversion results appear in the output, so an implementation
whose null pointer is not all-zero bits would produce identical text.
No address or pointer value is printed, and no pointer difference is formed anywhere in this
program, so ptrdiff_t width cannot reach the output at all. Plain char is never printed in a
signedness-dependent way. The only conversion specifiers this program uses are %d and
fixed-precision %.3f, both of which are target-width independent.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
